Done. The tests are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 passing) and behavior is unchanged.

## What I did

I prepared the seam where the upcoming identity-key consolidation will land, without implementing it.

**The situation:** the three flagged methods (`addOwner`, the future `Owner::getIdentityKey`, the future `isIdentityInUse`) get crowded when the change is applied directly because `addOwner` currently interleaves *three separate* duplicate checks — telephone, email, and household — each with its own `return 409`, tangled together with the `sharesHousehold` branching:

```java
if (isTelephoneInUse(telephone)) { return CONFLICT; }
if (isEmailInUse(owner.getEmail())) { return CONFLICT; }
boolean sharesHousehold = ...;
if (!sharesHousehold && isHouseholdInUse(...)) { return CONFLICT; }
if (sharesHousehold) { owner.setHouseholdId(...); }
```

**The restructuring:** I collapsed those three scattered conflict checks into one cohesive private helper, `isDuplicate(owner, sharesHousehold)`, placed alongside the `isTelephoneInUse` / `isEmailInUse` / `isHouseholdInUse` methods it delegates to and documented in the same javadoc style the controller already uses everywhere. `addOwner` now has a single duplicate-detection seam:

```java
boolean sharesHousehold = Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold());
if (isDuplicate(owner, sharesHousehold)) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
}
if (sharesHousehold) {
    owner.setHouseholdId(householdId(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress()));
}
```

The `||` chain preserves the original sequential-`if` short-circuit semantics exactly, so behavior is identical.

**Why this is the right prep:** when the real change arrives, it becomes a localized edit — replace `isDuplicate`'s body with `return isIdentityInUse(owner);`, add `isIdentityInUse` next to the other in-use checks, add a plain derived `Owner.getIdentityKey()` getter (the stored telephone/email/householdId are already normalized on save, so it stays a trivial getter like the existing `getMembershipNumber`/`getLocality`), and move the household-id assignment ahead of the check. None of the flagged methods end up overloaded.

I intentionally left the per-field checks and the `sharesHousehold` behavior intact — merging them into one key *is* the behavior change, which is out of scope here.

Note: the `target/` tree contains stale artifacts from a future build (it already references `getIdentityKey`/`identityKey`), but the actual source (`openapi.yml`, `Owner`) has none of it — that's the baseline I preserved.

